@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex overview
@cindex passkey
@cindex FIDO2

Passkeys are WebAuthn credentials: key pairs that a web site (a
@dfn{relying party}) registers instead of, or in addition to, a
password.  They are kept by an @dfn{authenticator}, usually a USB
security key.  gkey is an authenticator implemented in software on
the computer itself, which keeps the private keys inside the
computer's Trusted Platform Module (TPM).

gkey consists of two programs:

@table @command
@item gkey-service
A service, normally started with the user's graphical session, that
creates a virtual USB security key through the kernel's UHID driver
and answers the requests that web browsers send to it.  It speaks the
Client to Authenticator Protocol, version 2.0 (CTAP2), over the
CTAPHID transport, so any browser that supports FIDO2 security keys
can use it without further support.

@item gkey
A command line tool to list and delete the stored passkeys and to
inspect the TPM and the keystore.
@end table

gkey supports discoverable credentials (``passkeys'' proper, that let
you sign in without typing a user name) as well as server-side
credentials, the ES256 signature algorithm, and built-in user
verification through polkit.

@node Security model
@chapter Security model
@cindex security
@cindex TPM
@cindex root of trust

Every key gkey uses descends from a @dfn{storage primary key} that the
TPM derives, each time gkey connects to it, from the secret seed of
its owner hierarchy.  The seed never leaves the TPM, and nothing is
stored in the TPM's own non-volatile memory.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Each credential has its own ECC P-256 signing key, generated by the
TPM under the primary key.  The private part is returned only in a
form encrypted by the TPM (``wrapped''), and the key is created with
the @code{fixedTPM} and @code{fixedParent} attributes: it can be used
on this TPM only, and cannot be exported or duplicated.  Signing takes
place inside the TPM.

@item
The list of credentials (relying parties, user names, wrapped keys,
signature counters) is encrypted and authenticated with AES-256-GCM.
The 256-bit key is generated by the TPM and @dfn{sealed} to it, so the
keystore file is useless when copied to another machine, and any
modification of it is detected.

@item
Commands are sent to the TPM in a session salted with the primary key,
with parameter encryption, so that the sealed key does not cross the
bus between the processor and the TPM in the clear.

@item
Before a passkey is created or used, gkey asks polkit, whose
authentication agent in the user's desktop session asks for the
user's password (@pxref{User consent}).  gkey never sees the
password.
@end itemize

@cindex set-user-ID
Only the @samp{gkey} account may use the TPM and @file{/dev/uhid}.
@command{gkey-service} and @command{gkey} are installed set-user-ID
@samp{gkey}: each opens the devices it needs and connects to the
system bus, then gives up the account for good, keeping only the open
devices and bus connection, and only then reads or writes the
keystore, with the user's own permissions.  Programs running as the
user, malware included, can therefore neither use the TPM to unseal
the keystore or sign, nor create HID devices such as fake keyboards.
The kernel prevents them from debugging the set-user-ID processes or
reading their memory.

The bus remembers that the connection was made by @samp{gkey}, which
polkit's policy names as the owner of gkey's actions.  That lets the
programs describe each request in the authentication dialog, such as
the web site and account, or the passkeys about to be deleted.  Text
coming from web sites is shown with markup and control characters
neutralized.

Anyone may run the set-user-ID programs with any arguments and
environment.  They therefore refuse the options that would choose
another TPM connection, device or consent method
(@option{--tcti}, @option{--uhid}, @option{--verify=command}), and
ignore the environment variables that would (@env{GKEY_TCTI},
@env{TPM2TOOLS_TCTI}, @env{TCTI}, @env{DBUS_SYSTEM_BUS_ADDRESS} and
the @env{TSS2_*} variables of the TPM library).  They warn when the
user belongs to the @samp{tss} group, since programs running as such
a user can use the TPM directly.

Malware running as the user can still delete the keystore or put back
an older copy, since it is the user's file, but cannot use it.  It can
also send requests to the security key, through the browser's device
or its own copy of @command{gkey-service}, but each one needs the
user's password in the polkit dialog.  gkey cannot protect against an
attacker with root privileges, nor against programs in the @samp{tss}
group able to read the keystore.  Clearing the TPM (for instance from the
firmware setup) changes the owner seed and makes every stored passkey
permanently unusable; keep another way to sign in to important
accounts.

@node Setting up
@chapter Setting up
@cindex setting up
@cindex permissions

gkey-service runs as an ordinary user, as a systemd user service.
@samp{make install}, run as root, installs:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@command{gkey-service} and @command{gkey}, owned by the @samp{gkey}
account, with mode 4755 (set-user-ID).  It creates the
account with @command{systemd-sysusers} if needed.  When installing
into a staging directory (@env{DESTDIR}) or as an ordinary user, it
prints the commands to run as root instead.

@item
A udev rule granting the @samp{gkey} account @file{/dev/uhid}, and
@file{/dev/tpmrm0} through an access control list, which leaves the
distribution's own permissions (usually group @samp{tss}) in place.
The rule needs @command{setfacl}, from the @samp{acl} package.

@item
A @file{modules-load.d} file loading the @samp{uhid} kernel module at
boot, and the polkit actions, owned by the @samp{gkey} account.
@end itemize

Then apply the udev rule to the existing devices, make sure no user is
in the @samp{tss} group, and enable the service:

@example
sudo modprobe uhid
sudo udevadm control --reload
sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=tpmrm --subsystem-match=misc
sudo gpasswd -d $USER tss       # if you were a member
@end example

Log out and back in for the group changes to take effect, then:

@example
systemctl --user enable --now gkey.service
@end example

Users also need a polkit authentication agent in their desktop
session, as GNOME, KDE Plasma and most other desktops provide.  The
keystore is created when the first passkey is registered.  Browsers
then offer gkey like any other security key.

The service must not run with @code{NoNewPrivileges=}, or with any
systemd option that implies it in user units, such as
@code{SystemCallFilter=}: the kernel would ignore the set-user-ID
bit.

@cindex Nix
The Nix store cannot hold set-user-ID files.  On NixOS, install the
programs through @code{security.wrappers}, with
@code{setuid = true} and @code{owner = "gkey"}.

@node Invoking gkey-service
@chapter Invoking gkey-service
@cindex invoking @command{gkey-service}
@pindex gkey-service

@example
gkey-service [@var{option}]@dots{}
@end example

@command{gkey-service} creates the virtual security key and serves it
until it receives @code{SIGTERM} or @code{SIGINT}.

@table @option
@item -k @var{dir}
@itemx --keystore=@var{dir}
Keep the credentials in @var{dir} (@pxref{Files}).

@item -t @var{conf}
@itemx --tcti=@var{conf}
Reach the TPM through the TCTI (TPM Command Transmission Interface)
described by @var{conf}, such as @samp{device:/dev/tpmrm0} (the
default) or @samp{swtpm:host=localhost,port=2321} for a software TPM.
Without this option the environment variables @env{GKEY_TCTI},
@env{TPM2TOOLS_TCTI} and @env{TCTI} are consulted, in that order.
Refused, and the variables ignored, when the program runs
set-user-ID (@pxref{Security model}); it then always uses
@samp{device:/dev/tpmrm0}.

@item --uhid=@var{file}
Create the device through @var{file} instead of @file{/dev/uhid}.
Refused when the program runs set-user-ID.

@item --verify=@var{method}
Obtain user consent with @var{method}, @samp{polkit} (the default) or
@samp{command}.  @xref{User consent}.  The @samp{command} method is
refused when the program runs set-user-ID, since it would let anyone
grant consent.

@item --verify-command=@var{command}
Run @var{command} to obtain user consent.  Implies
@option{--verify=command}.

@item -v
@itemx --verbose
Print informational messages; give twice for debugging messages and
three times to trace every HID message.  The @env{GKEY_LOG}
environment variable accepts finer grained settings, in the syntax of
the Rust @code{env_logger} library.

@item -q
@itemx --quiet
Print only error messages.

@item --help
Display a summary of the options and exit.

@item --version
Display version information and exit.
@end table

The exit status is zero when the service stops because of a signal,
and non-zero when it fails.

@node Invoking gkey
@chapter Invoking gkey
@cindex invoking @command{gkey}
@pindex gkey

@example
gkey [@var{option}]@dots{} [@var{command} [@var{argument}]@dots{}]
@end example

@table @code
@item list
List the stored credentials: their ID, relying party, user, kind
(@samp{passkey} for discoverable credentials, @samp{server-side}
otherwise), creation date, and the number of times they were used.
This is the default command.

@item delete @var{id}@dots{}
Delete the credentials whose ID starts with each @var{id}.  Each
@var{id} must designate exactly one credential.  The relying party is
not told; remove the passkey from your account settings too.

@item info
Describe the TPM, the keystore and the authenticator.

@item init
Create an empty keystore.  @command{gkey-service} does so by itself
when needed.
@end table

@command{gkey} accepts the @option{--keystore}, @option{--tcti},
@option{--help} and @option{--version} options of
@command{gkey-service}.  It locks the keystore while it works, so it
can safely be used while the service runs.  The exit status is zero on
success and one on failure.

Every command first asks polkit to authorize the action
@samp{io.github.krishap_s.gkey.manage}, with a dialog describing what
it is about to do; @code{delete} names the passkeys it will delete.
When standard input is a terminal, @command{gkey} also starts
@command{pkttyagent}, polkit's text mode agent, which asks for the
password in the terminal if no graphical agent serves the session,
for instance over SSH.

There is no command to export credentials: their private keys cannot
leave the TPM.

@node User consent
@chapter User consent
@cindex user presence
@cindex user verification
@cindex polkit
@cindex PAM

FIDO distinguishes @dfn{user presence}, the user's consent to an
operation, from @dfn{user verification}, which also establishes who
the user is.  Relying parties ask for one or the other; gkey always
obtains at least the former.

With the default @samp{polkit} method, @command{gkey-service} asks
polkit to authorize one of these actions for its own process:

@table @samp
@item io.github.krishap_s.gkey.create-passkey
Creating a passkey.
@item io.github.krishap_s.gkey.sign-in
Signing in with a passkey.
@item io.github.krishap_s.gkey.reset
Deleting all passkeys, when the browser resets the security key.
@end table

polkit attributes the service, which runs outside of any login
session, to the user's graphical session, whose authentication agent
shows a dialog naming the web site and the account, and asks for the
user's password.  polkit checks it through PAM, so any PAM module configured
for polkit, such as a fingerprint reader, can be used.  polkit offers
no confirmation that does not authenticate, so the user authenticates
for user presence too.  The installed policy asks every time and never
remembers an authorization; administrators can change that with
polkit rules files.

polkit only accepts such a description from the owner of the action,
the @samp{gkey} account; when @command{gkey-service} does not run
set-user-ID, as in development, the dialog shows the fixed message of
the action instead.

With the @samp{command} method, the command given with
@option{--verify-command} is run by @file{/bin/sh}, with
@env{GKEY_REQUEST} set to @samp{presence} or @samp{verification},
@env{GKEY_OPERATION} to @samp{create}, @samp{sign-in} or @samp{reset},
and @env{GKEY_DESCRIPTION} set to a description of the request.  Exit
status zero grants the request.

The request is withdrawn when the browser cancels it.

@node Files
@chapter Files
@cindex files
@cindex keystore

@table @file
@item $XDG_DATA_HOME/gkey/keystore
The keystore, by default in @file{~/.local/share/gkey}.  The
@env{GKEY_KEYSTORE} environment variable and the @option{--keystore}
option select another directory.

@item $XDG_DATA_HOME/gkey/keystore.lock
Lock file used to serialize access to the keystore.

@item /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/io.github.krishap_s.gkey.policy
The polkit actions.

@item /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-gkey.rules
Grants the @samp{gkey} group access to @file{/dev/uhid} and
@file{/dev/tpmrm0}.

@item /usr/lib/sysusers.d/gkey.conf
Defines the @samp{gkey} account and group owning the programs.
@end table

